d}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{7}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2.\n$$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186124274 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/1c8969401a7c812e945fdc0b2f1774f6cd1d1d9c35269b2b20ea8165449d427f.png) be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/05f7384dd957b2616433104bfefbb8b3e7aaa619dbb1dff94fec43c8a99e6c3c.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186124274 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$." 13194139535858 false] [17592186124274 1145 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/be82bf02cdfe6a50557b312ba78708463248f844f81016df2cda41c6cfbb6c1a.png) such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ -x+y=k \\\\ 2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+4 & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =k \\\\ 0+4 & =k \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4.$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186124274 1145 "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + y \\\\ -x + y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 4; - x + y = k; 2y = 8\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4$, which we substitute into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 4 \\\\\nx + 4 &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + y &= k \\\\\n0 + 4 &= k \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4$." 13194139535858 false] [17592186124440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370699 13194139535858 true] [17592186124440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370739 13194139535858 true] [17592186124440 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370746 13194139535858 true] [17592186124441 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370706 13194139535858 true] [17592186124441 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370713 13194139535858 true] [17592186124441 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370726 13194139535858 true] [17592186124441 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370760 13194139535858 true] [17592186124468 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370692 13194139535858 true] [17592186124468 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370753 13194139535858 true] [17592186124468 1020 #edb/ref 17592186370767 13194139535858 true] [17592186125031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123208 13194139535858 false] [17592186125031 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370720 13194139535858 true] [17592186125031 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535858 true] [17592186125031 1100 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{3}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535858 false] [17592186125031 1100 "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125031 1145 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{11}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-11{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3}=11\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}-5(-1+{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2-{x}_{3} \\\\ -1+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125033 1017 #edb/ref 17592186123220 13194139535858 false] [17592186125033 1017 #edb/ref 17592186370733 13194139535858 true] [17592186125033 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535858 true] [17592186125033 1100 "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{4}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} - x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{2} - x_{1} + 3x_{3} + 4x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} - 3x_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535858 false] [17592186125033 1100 "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125033 1145 "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125048 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535858 true] [17592186125048 1100 "Let $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-2x + y \\\\ -x + 4y \\\\ -3x - 5y\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Im(T)$." 13194139535858 false] [17592186125048 1100 "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125048 1145 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125049 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535858 true] [17592186125049 1100 "Let $T = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Im(T)$." 13194139535858 false] [17592186125049 1100 "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125049 1145 "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186125066 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535858 true] [17592186125066 1100 "Given the transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y \\\\ y - x + 6z \\\\ 8z - x + 3y\\end{bmatrix}$, find its rank." 13194139535858 false] [17592186125066 1100 "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ y-x+6z \\\\ 8z-x+3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." 13194139535858 true] [17592186125066 1145 "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\