ow. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -28 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186370739 1113 #uuid "2dc778fa-5a47-56a3-8dad-82a1ece20129" 13194139535858 true] [17592186370739 1145 "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 & -28 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 & 26\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -4+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7+(-2)=-9.$" 13194139535858 true] [17592186370740 1000 #edb/ref 17592186370741 13194139535858 true] [17592186370740 1000 #edb/ref 1759218637074